course: dictionary
lesson: 3990
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. A dictionary lists each meaning separately, and the sentence around the word shows which one is meant. A galley is a kitchen on a ship, boat or plane. «The cook worked all night in the galley.» The sentence is about a cook on a ship, so galley means the kitchen.
explain: The same word can also name a long ship from long ago. «Mediterranean galleys were often rowed by prisoners, captives and enslaved people, on both Christian and Muslim sides.» The clue is rowed: these galleys were moved by oars, so here galley means a long ship.
explain: When you meet a word with several meanings, ask which meaning fits the clues. Then try it in the sentence. Words about food and cooking point to the kitchen. Words about oars and battles point to the ship. Words about checking mistakes in a book point to the printing meaning.
fact: A galley is a kitchen on a ship, boat or plane.
fact: In history, a galley was a long ship moved mainly by many oars, used by the ancient Greeks and Romans and in the Mediterranean.
fact: Galleys were often rowed by prisoners and enslaved people, which is a hard part of their history.
fact: In printing, a galley proof is a long first printing of a text, used to check for mistakes.
fact: A dictionary numbers the meanings of a word, and the context tells you which number to choose.
exercise: «The cook worked all night in the galley, making soup for the crew.» Where did the cook work? | In a library | In a field of corn | *In the ship's kitchen | On the deck, watching the sea
exercise: «Enslaved rowers sat in the galley, pulling heavy oars all day.» What kind of galley is this? | A small fishing boat with a sail | A kitchen on a plane | *A long ship moved by oars | A long first printing of a text
exercise: «The printer sent the galley to the author to check for mistakes.» What is a galley here? | *A long first printing used to check for mistakes | A trolley for food on a plane | A ship's kitchen | A rowing ship from ancient Rome
test: «The flight attendants heated the meals in the galley before lunch.» Where were the meals heated? | *On a plane, in the kitchen | In a printing works | In a Roman warship | In a castle
test: «The Romans sailed their galleys into battle, the oars beating together.» Which meaning fits? | A first printing of a text | A kitchen on a ship | *A long ship moved by oars | A type of sail
exam: «Ella read the galley and found three spelling mistakes before the book was printed.» What does galley mean here? | *A long first printing of a text used to check for mistakes | A kitchen on a ship | A trolley for food on a plane | A rowing ship from ancient times
